{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Learning Experience Design and Software Technology", "title": "LXD Optimizer Critical Use", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting lxd optimizer critical use to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology", "ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "06", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/02/05"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_calibration"]}, "content": "# LXD Optimizer Critical Use\n\n![LXD Optimizer Critical Use textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-06-lxd-optimizer-critical-use.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA new class of tools promises to \"optimize\" your learning experience: scan a module, score it on completion, time-on-page, and efficiency, and return a ranked list of edits with predicted gains. Used uncritically, these optimizers are dangerous precisely because they are useful. They surface real problems (a missing alt text, a confusing layout) alongside changes that move a metric while damaging learning, and they present both with the same confident green arrow. This section is about reading an optimizer report as a designer rather than obeying it as an operator.\n\nThe crux is the gap between a metric and the learning it stands in for. An optimizer can measure completion, dwell time, and click counts, but it cannot measure understanding, transfer, or whether a learner can perform the target action a week later. Every optimizer suggestion is therefore an argument about a proxy, and the designer's job is to ask, for each one, whether improving the proxy also improves, leaves untouched, or harms the actual learning goal. Goodhart's law looms over the whole enterprise: when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAn optimizer scans a module and returns four edits, each with a predicted gain: shorten the worked example from six steps to two (+14% completion), move the self-check above the reading (+9% time-on-page), add alt text to two diagrams (+3% completion), remove a rarely played audio-description track (+6% efficiency). The dashboard turns green if you apply all four. The tempting move, and the one a manager under deadline pressure may demand, is \"accept all, the numbers all went up.\"\n\nBut three of those four edits trade learning for a metric. Cutting the worked example to two steps removes the modeled reasoning the example exists to teach; the completion gain comes from learners finishing a worse artifact. Moving the self-check before the reading raises time-on-page because confused learners linger, not because they engage. Removing the audio-description track improves efficiency by deleting an accessibility provision the metric cannot value. Only the alt text serves both a learner need and the goal, and its completion gain is a side effect, not the reason to do it. The storyboard trace must record which suggestions were refused and why, so the green dashboard cannot stand in for a design decision.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nCritical use of an optimizer rests on understanding what a proxy metric can and cannot see. **Completion** measures whether learners reached the end, not whether they learned; it rises when you make a module shorter, easier, or less demanding, regardless of whether that helps. **Time-on-page** is ambiguous by design: it goes up with both deep engagement and total confusion, so it can never, on its own, tell you which one occurred. **Efficiency** rewards removing steps, which collides directly with provisions (accessibility tracks, worked examples) whose value is concentrated in a minority of learners or in long-term outcomes the metric never measures.\n\nThe disciplined response is to classify each suggestion by its relationship to the learning goal, not by the size of its predicted gain. A suggestion is **goal-aligned** (improves the proxy and the learning, like adding alt text), **goal-neutral** (improves the proxy without touching learning, where you may accept it for the side benefit), or **goal-opposed** (improves the proxy by harming learning, which you reject regardless of the number). The predicted gain is information about the proxy only; it carries no weight in the verdict until you know which of the three categories the suggestion falls into.\n\nThe course anchors are the instruments that let you classify:\n\n- **Cognitive load theory** distinguishes a cut that removes extraneous load (good) from one that removes the germane processing a worked example exists to create (bad), even though both raise completion.\n- **UDL learner variability** is the defense against efficiency edits, since usage-weighted metrics systematically undervalue features that serve learners with disabilities or atypical paths.\n- **Mayer's principles** tell you when a \"shorten it\" suggestion crosses from the coherence principle (cut the irrelevant) into deleting the explanatory core.\n\nEvery optimizer suggestion falls into one of three categories by its relationship to the learning goal. Match each category to the right verdict and to the example from the report.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Goal-aligned\",\"definition\":\"Improves the proxy and the learning, like adding alt text; accept it, treating the metric gain as a side effect\"},{\"term\":\"Goal-neutral\",\"definition\":\"Improves the proxy without touching learning; accept only for a stated side benefit\"},{\"term\":\"Goal-opposed\",\"definition\":\"Improves the proxy by harming learning, like cutting a worked example to two steps; reject regardless of the gain\"},{\"term\":\"Predicted gain size\",\"definition\":\"Information about the proxy only; carries no weight in the verdict until the category is known\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"An optimizer reports that shortening a worked example from six steps to two will raise completion by 14 percent, the largest gain on the report. How should the size of the gain affect your verdict?\" explanation=\"The magnitude is irrelevant until you know the mechanism. The completion gain here comes from learners finishing a worse artifact, because the cut removes the germane modeled reasoning the example exists to teach. A +14% from gutting the example is worse than a +3% from adding alt text. Read mechanism first, magnitude never first.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A larger gain is a stronger reason to accept, so apply it first\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The size is irrelevant until the mechanism is known; this gain comes from a worse artifact, so reject it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Accept it because completion is the most important learning metric\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Triaging an Optimizer Report\n\nAn LXD optimizer scanned one module and returned four suggestions. Here is the before-and-after of how a critical user handled them.\n\n**Before (accept-all framing)**\n\nThe designer's first instinct: \"The tool raised every metric, so apply all four edits.\"\n\n**The decision point.** Read against the learning goal, the four suggestions split apart:\n\n| Optimizer suggestion | Reported gain | Verdict and reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Shorten the worked example from 6 steps to 2 | +14% completion | Reject. Cutting the steps removes the modeled reasoning the example exists to teach. |\n| Move the self-check above the reading | +9% time-on-page | Reject. Higher time-on-page reflects confusion, not engagement, when the check precedes the content. |\n| Add alt text to two unlabeled diagrams | +3% completion | Accept. Serves accessibility and learning; the gain is a side effect, not the reason. |\n| Remove the rarely played audio-description track | +6% efficiency | Reject. The track is an accessibility provision the usage metric cannot value. |\n\n**After (critical-use framing)**\n\nThe designer applied one of four edits, kept a one-line reason for each verdict, and added a transfer quiz before and after the accepted change to confirm understanding did not drop.\n\nDesigner note (decision level): \"I refused to treat the optimizer's proxies as learning. I accepted only the edit that served both a learner need and the goal, and I logged why I rejected the three that moved a metric without serving learning, including one that would have cut an accessibility feature.\"\n\nThe revision is defensible because each optimizer suggestion was judged against the learning goal and learner variability, not accepted as a batch because the dashboard turned green.\n\nTriage the report one row at a time, predicting the verdict before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Triaging the optimizer report\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Row 2: move the self-check above the reading, predicted +9% time-on-page. What does the rising time-on-page most likely mean here?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Deeper engagement, so accept it\",\"Confused learners lingering because the check precedes the content, so reject it\",\"Faster completion, so accept it\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Time-on-page is ambiguous by design: it rises with both engagement and confusion. With the check before the content, the gain reflects confusion, not learning, so the suggestion is goal-opposed and rejected.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Row 4: remove the rarely played audio-description track, predicted +6% efficiency. On what grounds is this rejected?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is accepted, since few learners use it\",\"UDL: an efficiency metric structurally cannot see the value of a provision used by a few learners with high need\",\"It is neutral, so accept for the side benefit\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Usage-weighted metrics systematically undervalue accessibility provisions. The track serves a minority of learners whose need the proxy cannot measure, so removing it is goal-opposed regardless of the efficiency arrow.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Only the alt-text row survives, and the designer notes the completion gain is a side effect, not the reason. The defensible output is a per-row log of verdicts plus a transfer quiz before and after the accepted edit to confirm understanding did not drop. The green dashboard is an input, never the output.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nJudge each optimizer suggestion by its effect on the learning goal, not by the size of its predicted metric gain. The number tells you about the proxy; only the learning goal tells you whether to act.\n\nRun each suggestion in an optimizer report through this triage before accepting any of them:\n\n1. **Proxy named:** What exact metric does this suggestion move (completion, time, efficiency), and what does that metric fail to capture?\n2. **Mechanism:** Does the gain come from better learning, from an easier task, or from confused learners lingering?\n3. **Category:** Is the suggestion goal-aligned, goal-neutral, or goal-opposed?\n4. **Accessibility check:** Does the edit remove a provision that serves a minority of learners whose value the metric cannot see?\n\nAccept goal-aligned edits, accept goal-neutral edits only for a stated side benefit, and reject goal-opposed edits no matter how green the arrow. Then log the reason for each verdict, because the rejections are the evidence that a designer, not a dashboard, made the call.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading treats a larger predicted gain as a stronger reason to act. The size of the gain is irrelevant until you know its mechanism; a +14% completion that comes from gutting a worked example is worse than a +3% gain that comes from adding alt text. Read the mechanism, then the magnitude, never the reverse.\n\nThe second misreading assumes that because an optimizer is automated, it is objective. An optimizer is only as objective as its proxy metrics, and those metrics encode a value system that prizes completion and efficiency over transfer and equity. The tool is not neutral; it has a built-in bias toward shorter, easier, faster modules. Critical use means treating the report as an argument from a biased source, to be cross-examined against the learning goal, not as a verdict from a disinterested judge.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the optimizer table from the worked example one row at a time and watch the triage operate. The \"shorten the worked example\" row predicts +14% completion, the largest gain on the report, and is rejected first, which already breaks the accept-all instinct. The reason is mechanistic: a worked example reduces load for novices by modeling the full chain of reasoning, so cutting it to two steps removes the germane processing that is the example's entire purpose. The completion gain is real and irrelevant.\n\nNotice that the table separates the gain from the verdict in two columns. The \"self-check above the reading\" row is rejected because higher time-on-page here signals confusion, not engagement; reading the mechanism behind the metric flips its apparent meaning. The \"remove audio-description track\" row is rejected on UDL grounds, because an efficiency metric structurally cannot see the value of a provision used by a few learners with high need. Only the alt-text row survives, and the designer's note is careful to say the completion gain is a side effect, not the reason, which keeps the decision anchored to the goal.\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that a defensible response to an optimizer is a per-suggestion log of verdicts and reasons, plus a follow-up check (the transfer quiz before and after the accepted edit) that confirms understanding did not drop. The green dashboard is an input to that process, never its output.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, practice on real dashboards: for any analytics report you see (a course's completion stats, a website's bounce rate), name the proxy and then name one thing that proxy cannot see. The habit of asking \"what does this number fail to capture?\" is the entire defense against optimizer overreach, and it transfers far beyond learning design.\n\nA strong reader of optimizer reports can answer four questions about any suggested edit:\n\n1. Which proxy does this move, and what does that proxy fail to measure?\n2. Does the gain come from better learning, an easier task, or learner confusion?\n3. Is the suggestion goal-aligned, goal-neutral, or goal-opposed?\n4. What follow-up evidence would confirm that understanding did not drop when the metric rose?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section is a triaged optimizer report: every suggestion classified as goal-aligned, goal-neutral, or goal-opposed, each with a one-line mechanism and verdict, plus at least one reasoned rejection. The strongest versions add a before-and-after transfer check on the accepted edit, demonstrating that the designer confirmed learning rather than trusting the dashboard.\n\nWhen you bring an optimizer suggestion to BigAL, ask it to expose the proxy and the mechanism, not to confirm the gain. A useful prompt is: \"An optimizer says shortening my worked example will raise completion 14%. What is completion failing to measure here, and what learning would I lose?\" That turns the AI into a cross-examiner of the metric instead of a second voice telling you to apply the edit.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same four-suggestion report and change the optimizer's objective from completion and efficiency to a different proxy: predicted exam score from an embedded item bank. The triage logic survives, but the verdicts can move. Now the \"shorten the worked example\" suggestion might be reported as *lowering* predicted score, because the optimizer's new proxy partly captures the understanding the example builds; the metric and the goal are better aligned, so the tool's recommendation is more trustworthy, though still not authoritative. The lesson is that an optimizer is only as good as the distance between its proxy and your real goal. If you can explain why the audio-description edit is still rejected even under a learning-aligned proxy (because exam score still cannot see accessibility need), you understand that critical use is permanent, not a workaround for bad metrics.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"State Goodhart's law in your own words and explain why it makes optimizer dashboards permanently in need of critical use rather than fixable by a better metric.\" answer=\"Goodhart's law says that when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure, because people and tools optimize the proxy itself rather than the underlying goal it once tracked. An optimizer that targets completion or efficiency will find edits that move those numbers (shorter, easier modules) even when they harm learning. No metric fully captures understanding, transfer, or accessibility need, so any proxy made into a target drifts from the goal. That gap never closes, which is why critical use, judging each suggestion by its effect on the real goal, is permanent rather than a temporary patch for bad metrics.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-06-lxd-optimizer-critical-use-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 02.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use", "stem": "An LXD optimizer reports that shortening a worked example raised average time-on-page and completion rate. Why is this not sufficient reason to keep the change?", "options": ["Time-on-page and completion are engagement proxies, not measures of whether learners understood the worked example.", "Optimizers are always wrong and should be ignored.", "The change should be kept because higher completion is the goal of any course.", "Shorter worked examples are always better for learning."], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "Optimizers report proxies. A change can raise completion while reducing the generative processing a worked example is meant to support.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_metric_is_learning", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use", "stem": "An optimizer returns a list of 12 suggested edits. What is the critical-use move?", "options": ["Apply all 12 at once to save time, then re-run the optimizer.", "Triage the list: accept the edits that serve the learning goal, reject the ones that only move a proxy, and record a reason for each.", "Reject all 12 because automated tools cannot be trusted.", "Apply only the first three, since order signals importance."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Wholesale acceptance hides which edits served learning. Triaging each suggestion against the learning goal keeps the designer accountable.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_accept_all_suggestions", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use", "stem": "The optimizer flags an audio-description track as \"low-efficiency\" because few learners play it, and recommends removing it. What should the designer weigh that the optimizer cannot see?", "options": ["Nothing; low usage proves the track is unnecessary.", "The track is an accessibility provision for blind and low-vision learners, a learner-variability need the optimizer's usage metric cannot measure.", "Whether the audio file size slows page load.", "Whether the narrator's voice is pleasant."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The optimizer measures aggregate usage, not who depends on a feature. Accessibility provisions can be essential for a few learners while looking inefficient in aggregate.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_optimizer_knows_context", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_lxd_optimizer_critical_use"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "You accept an optimizer edit that raised completion by 18 percent. To make this a defensible evidence-based revision, what additional evidence should you gather?", "options": ["A second optimizer run confirming the completion gain.", "A learning measure (for example, a short transfer quiz before and after) showing whether understanding held or dropped when completion rose.", "Survey ratings of how modern the page looks.", "The exact timestamp of when the edit was applied."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A completion gain is only a proxy. Pairing it with a learning measure tests whether the optimizer's edit served the goal or just the metric.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_metric_is_learning", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_metric_is_learning", "pattern": "engagement_metric_proxy_for_learning", "description": "Treating an optimizer's engagement metric (time on page, clicks, completion) as a measure of learning", "trigger": "learner accepts an optimizer suggestion because it raises time-on-page or completion without asking whether learning improved", "feedback": "An optimizer reports proxies, not learning. Before accepting a suggestion, ask what learning outcome the metric stands in for and whether the change actually serves it.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_accept_all_suggestions", "pattern": "optimizer_output_accepted_wholesale", "description": "Accepting every optimizer recommendation as a batch instead of judging each against the learning goal", "trigger": "learner applies all suggested edits at once without separating the ones that serve learning from the ones that only serve the metric", "feedback": "Triage the optimizer's list. Accept the suggestions that serve the learning goal, reject the ones that only move a proxy, and record why for each.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_6_lxd_optimizer_critical_use_optimizer_knows_context", "pattern": "tool_assumed_to_know_context", "description": "Assuming the optimizer understands the learners, equity needs, and constraints that only the designer can see", "trigger": "learner removes a slow but accessible feature because the optimizer flagged it as low-efficiency", "feedback": "The optimizer does not see your learners or accessibility needs. Weigh its efficiency flags against learner variability the tool cannot measure.", "rail_action": "represent"}]}